\f4\fs26 Alexander Shakti was very surprised when the Commissioner handed him such a sensitive matter to investigate. The Commissioner had told him it was a simple murder-suicide, but he was not going to fall for that one again. He was still stinging from the Lee debacle and this time he was not going to make any mistakes. No slip-shod investigation for him. Every piece of information available would be gathered, logged, categorized, examined and re-examined. He would drown himself in a tsunami of data. He would check all the boxes and most of all he would cheat. Candid Criminal had some of the best surveillance equipment going around and Shakti had unrestricted access to the entire inventory.\
\'93Alex, the ear-piece is undetectable and quite comfortable. The microphone and camera are in these glasses and are already on. You do not have to adjust or fiddle with anything, okay.\'94 Cracker instructed as he handed the spectacles to him. \'93I can hear and see everything you do. I will tell you what to say. Simple.\'94\
\'93Thank you, Cracker. I am so very grateful.\'94 Shakti had hundreds of workers at his disposal to help him navigate the crime scene, nevertheless he called on the L.2. cleaner, the only person he could trust.\
Shakti arrived at Pre-emptive Reciprocity and was hurried to the director\'92s office. It had been cordoned off by the first responders and they had already called in the cavalry. There were investigators and machines of every kind taking samples, holo-grammetry, robotic vacuum cleaners scurrying about and little flying drones taking measurements and air samples. The bodies were still in-situ with the former Director slumped across his desk with a scorched entry wound in his forehead. Janine Wildsmith lay crumpled on her side with a gaping hole in her temple, skin still smoking from the photon blast. The gun, tightly grippped in her dead hand, clear testimony to her culpability. The forensic team were poking and prodding them with complete disregard to their dignity. With confidence and purpose Shakti marched to the bodies, making observations and taking notes, every once in while he changed his expression as if he had made some remarkable discovery.\
\'93I want the entire building locked down. No-one in or out and I want the names of every one who had access to this floor. Get all of their comms-records and computer activity for the last six months.\'94 Shakti announced to the room, a few heads turned but turned back in disinterest. \'93Who is the senior officer here?\'94\
\'93I am the new director. Damien Hardman, MBA. L.5.\'94 Damien was pleased to see that a senior investigator had finally arrived. He was not used to having to play second string to cops and from what he had seen so far they were altogether a lacklustre group with questionable intelligence. Shakti shot out his clammy right hand and shook Damien\'92s hand limply.\
\'93Lieutenant Alexander Shakti, L.5. Pleased to meet you.\'94 The new Director\'92s smile vanished upon hearing who had been sent to wash his dirty laundry. \
\'93Lieutenant Shakti.\'94 Damien did not know what else to say. Shakti\'92s was a legendary fuck-up and wondered how he was going to mess this one up. \'93I was one of the first to arrive and in my opinion the secretary shot the boss and then shot herself. We would like this matter to be handled sensitively. Obviously we can\'92t keep a lid on something like this, but we can control how the information is released.\'94 \
\'93I won\'92t be jumping to any conclusions on this one. I will treat it as if there are others involved. What can you tell me about the shooter?\'94 \
\'93I knew her well, she was a good woman. I had been speaking to here only minutes before and she was excited about her son\'92s up-coming birthday. I cannot understand what happened.\'94\
\'93Okay, I will arrange for you to give a holo-statement later, but in the mean-time could you arrange for the records I requested to be collected?\'94\
\'93Yes, of course. Agent Jones?\'94 He called out and Agent Jones was immediately by his side. \'93This is important. Have this building locked down and get the names of all the people who have access to this floor. I need all the communications and computer logs for everyone on the list for the last six months.\'94 \
\'93Yes, sir.\'94 Agent Jones ran his boney fingers over his bumpy head, through his this black locks and turned for the door. \'93I think we already locked it down.\'94\
\'93One more thing, Agent Jones.\'94\
\'93Yes, Director?\'94\
\'93Two cappuccinos. You want a coffee, Lieutenant? Yes? Two cappuccinos.\'94 Damien quietly smiled. \'93That\'92s how you deal with minions. Let them think they are doing something important and then bring them back to earth with the mundane.\'94 \
\'93Yes, fuck them. A good Policy.\'94 Shakti repeated what Cracker told him, not believing he had just released the \'93F\'94 word for the first time in his life.\
\'93Exactly, fuck them.\'94 Damien laughed and was amazed that, despite the rumour-mill, Shakti seemed almost competent and even likeable. \
\'93I don\'92t want to get in the way of your operations, but I will have to interview every person on this floor and anyone else who was here today. I would like some office space for a few weeks. Do you think you can swing it for me, Director Hardman?\'94\
\'93I will get Agent Jones onto it as soon as he appears with our coffee and please, call me Damien.\'94\
Shakti spent the next several hours inspecting raw data and the haul of samples taken from the Director\'92s office. He projected an air of confidence, asking salient questions of the forensic team and even throwing a few jokes in for good measure. Team members could not help but notice the dramatic change in their idiot boss. He seemed to know what he was doing. When the list of witnesses and suspects were transmitted to him, he quickly set to the task of prioritizing interviews and allocating resources skillfully and judiciously.\
Shakti knew that he could not interview for shit and decided to look for help. He had found it difficult enough to interview a witness to a traffic accident and he knew that he was no match for the army of agents, all trained in the art of deception. Although every request for assistance was met with an unambiguous, \'93No,\'94 he had heard of one Detective that was, reputedly, honest and quite the best interviewer in the service of the Dominion. She was also said to be a formidable character with impeccable ethics who could spot deception in an instant. Hayley Lanning was smart enough to take these agents on. \

